WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.789
Welcome to Agents, Tools, and Guardrails,
a practical beginner class.

2
00:00:03.789 --> 00:00:07.579
Today you will classify systems, design
boundaries, and inspect evidence.

3
00:00:07.579 --> 00:00:11.368
You will study one fictional refund using
copied Python dictionaries.

4
00:00:11.368 --> 00:00:15.158
Nothing here performs model inference,
network access, or real transactions.

5
00:00:15.158 --> 00:00:18.947
No external sandbox, production service,
payment, or deletion is involved.

6
00:00:18.947 --> 00:00:22.737
First, distinguish responses,
deterministic workflows, and bounded

7
00:00:22.737 --> 00:00:26.526
agentic loops carefully. Next, define
trusted state, tools, authority, checks,

8
00:00:26.526 --> 00:00:30.316
budgets, and exits. Then, trace typed
authorization before any copied

9
00:00:30.316 --> 00:00:34.105
state can change. You will see why Python
makes True

10
00:00:34.105 --> 00:00:37.895
compare equal to one. You will enforce
target, tenant, version,

11
00:00:37.895 --> 00:00:41.684
value, permission, and balance. You will
expose three defects inside

12
00:00:41.684 --> 00:00:45.474
an intentionally buggy executor. You will
compare exact receipts across

13
00:00:45.474 --> 00:00:49.263
success, denial, and recovery. You will
stop repetition, budget exhaustion,

14
00:00:49.263 --> 00:00:53.053
and unresolved uncertain outcomes. Later,
predict changed inputs before receiving

15
00:00:53.053 --> 00:00:56.842
separate corrective feedback yourself.
Every supplied transcript is expected

16
00:00:56.842 --> 00:01:00.632
evidence, not newly executed evidence.
Finish with records that support

17
00:01:00.632 --> 00:01:04.421
claims without inventing external
verification. Distinguish model calls,

18
00:01:04.421 --> 00:01:08.211
deterministic workflows, and agentic loops
by who controls the next

19
00:01:08.211 --> 00:01:12.000
step.

20
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Distinguish model calls, deterministic
workflows, and agentic loops by their

21
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.000
observable control flow. Classify each run
by control flow, not

22
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.000
its product label. The module states, “A
model response is

23
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.000
one generated output.” More completely,
that output answers one input

24
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.000
without continuing execution. The module
also states, “A deterministic workflow

25
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.000
follows steps selected.” Those steps are
selected by application code,

26
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.000
including fixed branches. An agentic loop
delegates some next-action selection

27
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.000
to a planner. The planner observes
results, updates state, and

28
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.000
may continue working. Consider routing a
support ticket to an

29
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.000
appropriate specialist queue. Suggesting
one queue name is simply a

30
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.000
single model response. Applying a category
lookup table is a

31
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.000
deterministic coded workflow. Inspecting
records adaptively, then choosing another

32
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.000
inspection, forms a loop. Adaptation may
handle exceptions, but adds

33
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.000
cost and failure paths. Fixed workflows
are preferable whenever they

34
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.000
reliably satisfy requirements already.
Loops should earn their complexity through

35
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.000
measurable adaptive value first. Ask who
chooses actions, retains state,

36
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.000
and declares completion explicitly.
Agentic behavior never proves broad

37
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.000
authority, reliability, or production
safety.

38
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.235
Apply those categories to a fictional
customer password-reset request today.

39
00:02:28.235 --> 00:02:32.471
A response can explain policy but cannot
complete the reset.

40
00:02:32.471 --> 00:02:36.706
A deterministic workflow verifies
identity, issues one link, then stops.

41
00:02:36.706 --> 00:02:40.941
Its code fixes ordering, branching,
expiration, logging, and rate limits.

42
00:02:40.941 --> 00:02:45.176
A loop could choose recovery tools after
observing account evidence.

43
00:02:45.176 --> 00:02:49.412
That flexibility helps only when
authorized exceptions genuinely require

44
00:02:49.412 --> 00:02:53.647
adaptation. Otherwise, extra choices
increase testing effort and uncertain

45
00:02:53.647 --> 00:02:57.882
intermediate states. Identity verification
and issuance authority must remain outside

46
00:02:57.882 --> 00:03:02.118
any planner. The planner may propose
actions, but proposals never

47
00:03:02.118 --> 00:03:06.353
grant permission themselves. Compare this
with our scripted refund planner

48
00:03:06.353 --> 00:03:10.588
used later today. It selects a
predetermined proposal, so no

49
00:03:10.588 --> 00:03:14.824
model inference occurs. The surrounding
executor still demonstrates boundaries

50
00:03:14.824 --> 00:03:19.059
required around adaptive planners. Prefer
deterministic control for stable,

51
00:03:19.059 --> 00:03:23.294
well-defined, consequential operations
whenever possible. Introduce a loop only

52
00:03:23.294 --> 00:03:27.529
for bounded choices that improve outcomes.
Measure improvement against cost,

53
00:03:27.529 --> 00:03:31.765
failures, recovery burden, and human
workload. The safest useful design

54
00:03:31.765 --> 00:03:36.000
is the simplest sufficient design
available.

55
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.222
Before execution, translate intentions
into an explicit run contract first.

56
00:03:40.222 --> 00:03:44.444
Goal names the bounded outcome and
observable completion condition precisely.

57
00:03:44.444 --> 00:03:48.667
State identifies trusted inputs, permitted
memory, receipts, and versions explicitly.

58
00:03:48.667 --> 00:03:52.889
Tools distinguish read-only observation
from actions that change state clearly.

59
00:03:52.889 --> 00:03:57.111
Authority binds identities, tenants,
targets, permissions, approvals, and

60
00:03:57.111 --> 00:04:01.333
values exactly. Checks cover typed
preconditions and independently observed

61
00:04:01.333 --> 00:04:05.556
final postconditions afterward. Budgets
limit steps and cost before each

62
00:04:05.556 --> 00:04:09.778
operation is charged. Exit rules cover
success, denial, invalidity, repetition,

63
00:04:09.778 --> 00:04:14.000
uncertainty, and handoff. Our goal refunds
exactly twenty-five on synthetic

64
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:18.222
ticket T-100 once. Trusted state names
tenant acme, ticket version

65
00:04:18.222 --> 00:04:22.444
three, and permissions. Approval binds
action, ticket, tenant, version, and

66
00:04:22.444 --> 00:04:26.667
value completely together. The fixture
allows four steps and six

67
00:04:26.667 --> 00:04:30.889
cost units maximum. Observation, proposal
evaluation, action attempts, and

68
00:04:30.889 --> 00:04:35.111
reconciliation each cost one. Available
tools are observe, copied refund,

69
00:04:35.111 --> 00:04:39.333
and observe-again operations only. The
planner proposes; an independent executor

70
00:04:39.333 --> 00:04:43.556
authorizes, mutates, and verifies
outcomes. Recovery preserves receipts and

71
00:04:43.556 --> 00:04:47.778
resumes only from newly verified state. A
run contract converts

72
00:04:47.778 --> 00:04:52.000
vague safety hopes into testable
boundaries.

73
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.235
Choose one bounded task that might use
several allowed actions.

74
00:04:56.235 --> 00:05:00.471
Write an observable condition proving that
task is complete successfully.

75
00:05:00.471 --> 00:05:04.706
Identify trusted state and anything the
planner must not alter.

76
00:05:04.706 --> 00:05:08.941
Name one read-only tool and one
consequential action tool explicitly.

77
00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:13.176
State whose identity and tenant authorize
that consequential action specifically.

78
00:05:13.176 --> 00:05:17.412
Bind approval to target, version, action,
and exact value completely.

79
00:05:17.412 --> 00:05:21.647
Add a maximum step budget and maximum cost
budget separately.

80
00:05:21.647 --> 00:05:25.882
Define repeated non-progress using
unchanged evidence, not elapsed time

81
00:05:25.882 --> 00:05:30.118
alone. Name one uncertain outcome
requiring reconciliation before any retry

82
00:05:30.118 --> 00:05:34.353
occurs. Specify what evidence a human
handoff must receive for

83
00:05:34.353 --> 00:05:38.588
